/** The guide's pages, in the order the docs site lists them. */
export const GUIDE_PAGES: readonly GuidePage[] = [
  {
    id: "overview",
    title: "Overview",
    description: "Start here. What Oppenheimer is, its parts (workspace, hosts, sessions, projects, tasks, bots, automations, inbox) and how they fit, and a map of the console's sections and Settings pages.",
    body: "Oppenheimer is a browser console for running coding agents on machines you own. You connect a machine (a **host**), and Oppenheimer starts **sessions** on it: each session is a terminal on that host, usually in a fresh git worktree, with a coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok, Pi or Oh My Pi) or a plain shell running in it. The terminal is streamed to your browser, so you can watch and type from a laptop or a phone, and the agent keeps working when you close the tab.\n\nAround sessions sit the things that organise and automate the work: **projects** and **tasks** to plan it, **automations** that start sessions on a schedule or a GitHub event, **bots** (shown in the console as **Employees**) that you chat with and that can start and follow sessions for you, and an **Inbox** that collects everything waiting on you.\n\n## The parts\n\n| Part | What it is | Read |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Workspace | Your personal space: hosts, projects, sessions, bots and history. One person per workspace; there are no teams, members or invitations. | this page |\n| Host | A macOS, Debian or Ubuntu machine you own, running the `runner` agent. Nothing on it is exposed to the internet: the runner dials out to Oppenheimer. | [Hosts](./hosts.md) |\n| Session | A terminal on a host, in a new git worktree, in a folder already on the host, or in a vendor's cloud. Window 0 runs the agent. | [Sessions](./sessions.md) |\n| Project | A saved scope: a name, repositories with their branches, a default host and agent. Sessions, tasks and automations are filed under one. | [Projects and tasks](./projects-and-tasks.md) |\n| Task | A card on Plan's board. A task can start a session or link existing ones. | [Projects and tasks](./projects-and-tasks.md) |\n| Bot | An LLM worker you chat with. It runs inside Oppenheimer (not on a host), has no shell, and does code work by starting sessions on your hosts. | [Bots](./bots.md) |\n| Routine and watch | How a bot works without being written to: on a schedule, on an event, or when a session or pull request it follows changes. | [Routines and watches](./routines-and-watches.md) |\n| Automation | A saved prompt with a project, host, agent and triggers. Each run is an ordinary session. | [Automations](./automations.md) |\n| Inbox | One list of what needs you: waiting sessions, bot approvals, pull requests, failed runs, notices. | [Inbox](./inbox.md) |\n| GitHub | The Oppenheimer GitHub App gives sessions access to your repositories; the Pull requests section is a review queue. | [GitHub](./github.md) |\n| CLI and MCP | `opp` and an MCP server drive the same things from scripts and other agents. | [CLI and MCP](./cli-and-mcp.md) |\n\nHow they fit: a session always runs somewhere you own (a host) or at a vendor you hold a key for. A person, a task, an automation, a bot or `opp` can start it, and it is the same kind of session in every case. Bots and automations never get more access than you have: they act as you.\n\n## The console\n\nThe console is a rail of sections on the left, a sidebar for the section you are in, and the main pane.\n\n| Rail item | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Employees** | Your bots: a sidebar of bots in groups, a chat with each, and each bot's settings. |\n| **Sessions** | New session, and the sidebar of your sessions grouped by project, with Pinned at the top. |\n| **Pull requests** | The PR queue, Analytics, and review sessions under For review. |\n| **Automations** | The automations list by project, their runs and run history. |\n| **Plan** | Inbox, Tasks (the board), Milestones, Calendar and Projects. |\n\nYou can drag the rail items into another order; the order is kept on that device. The console opens on **Plan → Inbox** unless you dragged another item to the top, in which case it opens on that one. Sessions, Automations, Plan and Pull requests can also list bots you place there, under a Bots heading in their sidebar; clicking one opens its chat without leaving the section.\n\n**Settings** opens from the account menu and has these pages:\n\n| Page | Use it to |\n| --- | --- |\n| Profile | Change your name, picture, username, email, password, signed-in devices, or delete the account. |\n| Memory | Write **About you**, which every bot reads, and see, edit or forget what your bots learned about you. |\n| Hosts | See, rename, update and remove hosts; Add host; open a blank terminal on a host. |\n| Watched repositories | Pick repositories whose open pull requests fill the Watching tab of the PR queue. |\n| Model providers | Add the API keys (or a ChatGPT sign-in) your bots run on. |\n| Cloud agents | Connect a Cursor API key to run sessions in Cursor's cloud. |\n| Mail | Connect Gmail accounts your bots may read and, with your approval, send from. |\n| WhatsApp | Link a spare WhatsApp number on a host so bots can read and send messages. |\n| Telegram | Connect a Telegram bot you made with @BotFather on a host so your bots can read and answer its chats. |\n| About | The versions of the console and the API. |\n\nA command palette (\"Search pages and actions…\") jumps to pages and actions. Prose fields (the new-session box, a bot's composer, review comments) have a microphone button for dictation when the deployment has a speech provider set up.\n\n## Getting started\n\nSigning up (GitHub, Google, or email and password) opens a short first-run flow:\n\n1. **Name your workspace** and pick its address. The name can change later; the address is permanent.\n2. **Connect GitHub**: install the Oppenheimer GitHub App on your account or an organization, with all or selected repositories. You can skip this and do it later from a repository picker.\n3. **Add your first host**: copy a prompt for a coding agent already on that machine, or run the install command yourself. You can skip this too.\n4. **You're all set**: a summary of the workspace, GitHub and host, then Go to the console.\n\nAfter that, a typical first hour is: open **Sessions → New session**, pick the host, a repository and an agent, describe the task and send. Sign the agent in once in its terminal if the host has never used it. Then try a bot in **Employees**: create one, tell it its job in one message, and it writes its own instructions.\n\n## What Oppenheimer does not do yet\n\n- No virtual machines or containers: sessions run directly on your hosts, as your user.\n- No teams: one person per workspace, and no way to invite others except sharing a single session's terminal.\n- Bots cannot run commands or read files themselves; they delegate to sessions.\n- No Slack integration, and no channel through which other people write to a bot.",
  },
  {
    id: "hosts",
    title: "Hosts",
    description: "Connecting a machine you own as a host (supported systems, pairing, the install, the user service, automatic updates), the Settings → Hosts page, agent CLIs on a host, what offline means, and removing or uninstalling a host.",
    body: "A **host** is a machine you own that runs Oppenheimer's host agent, the `runner`. Every session that is not a cloud session runs on a host, as your user, with the host's own tools and logins. A host belongs to you, not to a workspace.\n\nThe runner holds one outbound connection to Oppenheimer. It opens no port, and nothing on the host is reachable from the internet. The console reaches the terminal through that connection, or, when the network allows it, through a direct encrypted browser-to-host channel that falls back to the relay.\n\n## What a host needs\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n| --- | --- |\n| System | macOS, Debian or Ubuntu, on arm64 or amd64. Nothing else is supported. |\n| User | An ordinary user account. The installer refuses to run as root. |\n| Tools | `git` and `tmux`. The installer offers to install them with Homebrew or apt, but only on a typed yes; without them it stops before using the token. |\n| A lasting machine | The runner refuses machines that look temporary (Docker or Podman containers, CI, Codespaces, dev containers, Kubernetes pods). |\n| Agent CLIs | Optional at install: Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok, Pi, Oh My Pi (`omp`, which needs Bun) and GitHub's `gh`. The default is Claude Code and `gh`. |\n\nSessions are capped at 20 per host by default.\n\n## Adding a host\n\nOpen **Add host** from **Settings → Hosts**, from the host picker in New session (Add host…), or during first-run. Each opens a **one-hour, single-use registration token**. You can hold at most five unspent tokens; **New token** replaces the one on screen and the old one stops working at once.\n\nThere are two ways to use it:\n\n- **Copy prompt** (the default): paste the prompt into a coding agent (Claude Code, Codex) already running on the machine you want. The agent first checks it is on the right machine and asks you where session code should live and which agent CLIs to install, then runs the install and reads back `runner status`.\n- **Install manually instead**: run the one-line `curl … install.sh | … sh` command in a terminal on the machine yourself. The token rides in the environment, not as an argument. The installer's SHA-256 is shown under the command if you want to download and check it first. Settings' Add a host page offers both as an Install format choice, Command or Agent prompt.\n\nThe **Connection** list then moves from \"Runner installed\" to \"Host connected\", and the host appears in Settings → Hosts. Every pairing also emails you the machine's name and the start of its key fingerprint, so you notice a pairing you did not make.\n\nWhat the installer does, in order: checks the system, asks its questions up front, downloads the release and verifies its checksum (and its signature where OpenSSL 3 is present), places the binary under `~/.oppenheimer/bin` with an `oppenheimer-runner` command in `~/.local/bin`, registers the host, installs the user service, installs the chosen agent CLIs, and prints `runner status`. Re-running the same command repairs a half-finished install instead of adding a second host.\n\n## The service\n\n- **macOS**: a launchd user agent that starts at login and is kept alive.\n- **Linux**: a systemd user unit with lingering enabled, so it survives logout and starts at boot.\n\nSessions live in a dedicated tmux server, not in the runner process, so restarting or updating the runner does not interrupt an agent.\n\n## Where things live on a host\n\n| Place | Holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| `~/.oppenheimer/` | The runner's identity, key, binaries, logs and state. Never where an agent works. |\n| `~/oppenheimer/` (the work folder, chosen at install) | Repository stores and session checkouts, under your account and workspace. |\n\n`oppenheimer-runner workspaces --set DIR` moves the work folder for new sessions; it refuses while a session's checkout is still in the old one. Uninstalling never touches the work folder.\n\n## Settings → Hosts\n\nEach host is a card with:\n\n- its name (Rename in its menu; the name is display only);\n- platform, CPU count, runner version, and the Claude account its Claude Code is signed in to, when it reports one;\n- the city and country of its connecting address;\n- a status: **Running · N sessions**, **Idle** or **Offline**, with \"connected\" or \"last seen …\".\n\nThe card's menu also has **Update runner**, **Open terminal** (a blank terminal in your home directory on that host, as a session) and **Remove host**.\n\nAn online host's card also has **Desktop**, which opens the host's own screen (below).\n\n## A host's desktop\n\nYou can watch and drive a host's own screen from the console: from **Settings → Hosts → Desktop** on the host's card, or from a session's preview pane (the panel button in the session's bar), in its **Desktop** tab, which shows the screen of the session's host.\n\nThe host's owner decides whether its screen is shared, on the host itself:\n\n- `runner desktop on` shares it; `runner desktop off` stops, and closes a desktop already open in the console within a couple of seconds; `runner desktop` says how it stands. Nothing in the console turns it on.\n- The runner does not start a screen: it uses the screen-sharing server the machine already runs on its loopback, `127.0.0.1:5900` unless `--port` names another. On a Mac that is **Screen Sharing** (System Settings → General → Sharing); on Linux a VNC server such as TigerVNC or x11vnc listening on `127.0.0.1`.\n- If the server asks for a password, give the runner a file holding it, readable by that account only: `runner desktop on --password-file <file>`. macOS Screen Sharing signs in a macOS account: add `--username <account>`. The password stays on the host; the browser is never asked for one.\n\nThe desktop opens **watching**: you see the screen, and your clicks and keys reach nothing. **Take control** lets them through, until **Stop controlling**; the host itself drops your input while you only watch. Your session on the screen is shared, so watching never disconnects anyone else using it. **Full screen** gives the desktop the whole window; Escape leaves it.\n\nIn the console you open a desktop as yourself. An [API token](./cli-and-mcp.md) needs the **Host desktops** permission (`host-desktops:write`), which no other permission includes: a token that manages hosts (`hosts:write`) cannot open their screens.\n\nWhen the desktop cannot open, it says why: the host does not share its desktop (with the command to run there), no screen-sharing server answers on the host, or the server refused the host's sign-in. Fix it on the host, then **Try again**. An offline host's desktop comes back when the host reconnects.\n\n\n## Agents on a host\n\nEach agent runs from the host's own installation and its own login. You sign in once per host by starting a session with that agent and following its sign-in in the terminal; a sign-in link the agent prints becomes a button. Oppenheimer never stores an agent's credentials.\n\nFrom the host you can install more later: `oppenheimer-runner agents install claude` (or `codex`, `opencode`, `grok`), and `oppenheimer-runner tools install gh`. These install in your home directory without sudo. `git` and `tmux` stay with your package manager. The runner updates installed agent CLIs on its own about hourly unless `RUNNER_AGENT_UPDATES=off` is set before install. `oppenheimer-runner tools` lists what is installed.\n\n## Updates\n\nThe runner keeps itself on the current signed release. It checks at boot and every six hours, then waits for a quiet moment (no agent working, no terminal attached) for up to 24 hours before updating; urgent updates apply within 15 minutes. A new version that will not stay up is rolled back automatically. Sessions keep running through an update; open terminals reconnect in a few seconds.\n\nTo update now, use **Update runner** in the host's menu (only while the host is online), or `oppenheimer-runner update` on the host. `oppenheimer-runner update --pin VERSION` freezes a host on a version; a pinned host refuses Update runner until `--unpin`. A bot can ask for the same update with its `update_host_runner` tool, after your approval.\n\n## What offline means\n\nA host is **Offline** when its runner is not connected: the machine is off or asleep, the network is down, or the runner stopped.\n\n- Sessions already running keep running in tmux on the host if only the connection is lost. The terminal shows \"Host offline\" and reconnects on its own, catching up on output.\n- A session you start on an offline host waits as **Queued** and starts when the runner reconnects.\n- After a **reboot**, sessions come back stopped, with a **Restart** button that reopens the agent in the same worktree. A session marked kept (`runner sessions keep`, run from inside it) restarts on its own.\n- Automations defer runs aimed at an offline host; bots check that a host is online before starting work.\n\nWhen a terminal says the host is offline, **How to fix** gives the commands to run on the host (`oppenheimer-runner status`, the log, a reinstall) and a troubleshooting prompt you can hand to an agent there.\n\n## Removing a host\n\n**Remove host** in Settings → Hosts stops every session running on it (their logs are kept), revokes the runner's credential, and pauses automations aimed at it. Tick **Also delete everything on this machine** to also close every session and delete its worktree, uncommitted work included, with nothing pushed; that needs the host online.\n\nOn the host, `oppenheimer-runner uninstall` stops the service, unpairs the host and erases its identity. It refuses while sessions run unless `--force`. If Oppenheimer could not be told, it exits with code 7 and you finish it with Remove in Settings → Hosts. Binaries, logs and the work folder are left for you to delete.\n\nTo pair the same machine again, use Add host. If a host key may have been stolen, remove the host and pair it again.",
  },
  {
    id: "sessions",
    title: "Sessions",
    description: "Starting a session (project, where it runs, host, repositories, agent, model, permission level), the terminal and Chat views, the state dot, the Browser and Simulator preview, sharing a session, stopping, restarting and deleting it (what happens to the branch and worktree), cloud sessions, kept sessions and child sessions.",
    body: "A **session** is a terminal on one of your hosts with a coding agent in it. The runner makes a folder for it, starts a tmux session there, launches the agent in window 0, and streams the terminal to your browser. Closing the tab does not stop it.\n\n## Starting a session\n\nOpen **Sessions → New session**. The page is one box (\"What should we work on?\") with the project above it and a row of choices under it. Each choice opens on what you picked last time; picking a project fills in that project's defaults.\n\n| Choice | Options |\n| --- | --- |\n| Project | Any project, or Unassigned. New project… at the foot. |\n| Where it runs (one chip with the host, e.g. \"mac-studio · New worktree\") | **New worktree**: clone a repository onto a fresh branch. **Folder on host**: open a directory already on the host as it is; nothing is cloned. **Cloud**: Claude Code's or Codex's cloud started from a host, or Cursor's cloud (below). |\n| Host | Your hosts with their online state. An offline host is allowed: the session waits as Queued. |\n| Repositories | For a new worktree, one to five repositories, each on its own base branch. The agent starts in the first. \"Manage repository access\" opens the GitHub App's settings. |\n| Agent and model | Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok, Pi, Oh My Pi, or **Blank terminal** (a login shell, nothing launched). Each agent lists its models; older ones are under More models. Star a model to keep it under Favourites. |\n| Permission level | **Ask for approval** (the agent asks before it runs anything), **Approve for me** (edits and safe commands run, the rest asks; the default), **Full access** (nothing is asked; it can change the machine unattended). Hidden for a blank terminal. |\n| Effort | How long the agent may think, where the model supports levels. |\n\nWrite the first task and press Send. You can attach files to the first task (images, PDFs, text files, up to 5 MB each). For a blank terminal the text only names the session.\n\nThe host starts preparing the clone as soon as a host and repository are picked; a repository's first session on a host takes longer. While it starts, the pane shows the steps (connect, clone, check out, start the agent) and which one failed, if any.\n\nA worktree session works on a new branch, `oppenheimer/<slug>`, cut from the base you chose, with git access through one-hour tokens scoped to its repositories ([GitHub](./github.md)).\n\nA **folder session** works in your own folder, on whatever branch it is on, with your own git credentials. Oppenheimer never writes into or deletes that folder.\n\n## The sidebar\n\nThe Sessions sidebar lists sessions under their projects, with **Pinned** at the top, a search, filters (project, repository, agent, host) and a sort. The repository filter takes several repositories at once and lists a session that checks out any of them; each one shows as its own chip. A row's menu has **Rename**, **Hide**, **Move to project…**, **Pin**, **Share…** and **Delete**; Select acts on several rows at once.\n\n### Hidden sessions\n\nA hidden session keeps running but stays out of the sidebar. You hide one from its row's menu. A bot, a script or an agent can also start one hidden: `start_session` with `hidden` for a bot or over MCP, `opp sessions run --hidden` from the CLI. To see hidden sessions, turn on **Show hidden sessions** in the filter menu, which also counts them. They are listed with an eye-off icon, and **Show** in a row's menu brings that session back for good. Every device sees the same hidden sessions, and the toggle is kept per device.\n\n## The state dot\n\nEach session shows a state, read from the agent's own status report where it gives one (Claude Code) and otherwise from its screen:\n\n| State | Means |\n| --- | --- |\n| Starting, Queued | The host is creating it, or it waits for an offline host. |\n| Running | The agent is working. |\n| Needs input | The agent waits on you: a permission, a question, a sign-in. A note says which. |\n| Ready for review, Idle | The agent finished and waits at its prompt. |\n| Failed | The host could not start it. |\n| Stopped | Nothing runs; the worktree is kept. |\n\nSessions that need input, failed, or have not started after a minute also appear in the [Inbox](./inbox.md).\n\n## Terminal and Chat\n\nThe session pane has a bar with the name, the state, **Terminal / Chat**, the preview toggle and **Share**.\n\n- **Terminal** (the default) is the agent's real terminal. Type into it as you would locally. A plain drag selects and copies text. Paste or drop files onto it and their paths land in the agent's prompt.\n- **Chat** shows the same conversation read from the agent's transcript on the host: your messages, the agent's replies, its tool calls grouped (\"read 3 files, ran 2 commands\"), images it read, and files it shared. You can write to the agent and attach files from the chat's composer. Chat works for Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Grok; Pi and Oh My Pi are terminal only for now, and a blank terminal has none.\n\nThe console shows window 0, the agent's window. Extra shell windows can be made from the host with `runner sessions window`, but the console has no tab strip for them yet.\n\n## Preview: Browser and Simulator\n\nThe preview toggle opens a pane beside the terminal:\n\n- **Browser** shows a dev server the session runs, through the host's runner: pick any port the session listens on from 1024 up; the tab opens on the likeliest one (5173, 3000…). The app sees `localhost`. It shows \"Waiting for a dev server\" until one starts, and needs the deployment to have a preview domain configured.\n- **Simulator** shows an iOS simulator or Android emulator the host offers to its sessions, which you can watch and tap. It appears only on hosts set up with devices.\n\n## Sharing a session\n\n**Share** (in the session bar or the row's menu) gives one address per session, `/s/…`.\n\n- **Who**: specific people by email, or anyone with the link; either way they must sign in to Oppenheimer.\n- **Can view**: watch live, for 1 hour up to 7 days, or until you stop.\n- **Can write**: type and send prompts as you, with your credentials. Named people only, at most 24 hours, after you confirm \"I understand they act as me on this host\".\n- Guests see the terminal and the chat. **Stop sharing** ends access at once.\n\n## Stopping, restarting, deleting\n\n- **Stopped**: the agent and terminal end, the checkout stays. **Restart** brings the agent back in the same folder, resuming its conversation.\n- **Delete** stops the session, pushes each checkout's branch if it has commits, removes the worktree and session folder from the host, and drops the transcript. Commits already pushed stay on the remote. If there is work that is not pushed, delete stops unless you tick **Also discard work that is not pushed**. Oppenheimer never commits for you.\n- Deleting a folder session only ends its terminal; the folder stays. If the host is offline, the session is removed when the host is back.\n\nA host reboot leaves sessions stopped, waiting for Restart. A session marked **kept** comes back on its own after a reboot; keeping is set from inside the session with `runner sessions keep` (there is no console switch).\n\n## Cloud sessions\n\nUnder **Cloud** in the where-it-runs chip:\n\n- **Claude Code**: a host starts it with `claude --cloud`, under the Claude account that host is signed in to; it runs on claude.ai/code. Its state comes from the Oppenheimer Chrome extension while you are signed in to claude.ai. **Continue on a host** brings it back as a worktree session.\n- **Codex**: a host starts a Codex cloud task and reports its status while online. In the Codex environment chip, **Default** runs it in Codex's default environment with the repository; to use another, paste its id from ChatGPT Settings → Codex Cloud. The host's `codex` must be signed in with ChatGPT (`codex login`).\n- **Cursor**: runs in Cursor's cloud on your own key, no host. Connect the key in **Settings → Cloud agents**.\n\nA cloud session needs a task and has no terminal here. Follow-ups go to the vendor, except Cursor's, which take one through Oppenheimer after the last run ends. Deleting one removes it from Oppenheimer only.\n\n**The chat of a Claude Code cloud session.** A Claude Code cloud session that Oppenheimer started can show its chat in the session's pane, and take your replies, once its repository carries Oppenheimer's two hooks: one sends the session's conversation to Oppenheimer, the other collects what you type. Setting the hooks up is not in the console yet. Until the first line arrives, the pane is the usual cloud pane with its claude.ai link. A reply is handed to the agent when its current turn ends. If the session is idle, so nothing takes the reply within 15 seconds, one of your online hosts signed in to the same Claude account as the host that started the session sends it with `claude -p … --cloud`, and the session wakes up and answers. The chat shows that reply as sent until the agent picks it up. With no such host online, the reply waits for the session's next turn. Stop does nothing on a cloud session, and a session started on claude.ai rather than from Oppenheimer shows no chat here.\n\n## Child sessions and in-session tools\n\nAn agent inside a session has a `runner` command (its manual is `runner --skill`). With it the agent can start helper agents on the same host as **child sessions** (`runner sessions run`), message and wait on them, show you a file in the session's chat (`send-file`), send you a line in the Inbox (`notify`), be woken when its pull request's checks finish or it is reviewed (`watch-pr`), and set a wake-up up to 7 days ahead (`wake-in`). Child sessions run on the host, end when their parent ends unless started with `--outlive-parent`, and are not shown in the console's sidebar; the parent agent manages and closes them. To start work on another host, an agent uses `opp` ([CLI and MCP](./cli-and-mcp.md)).",
  },
  {
    id: "projects-and-tasks",
    title: "Projects and tasks",
    description: "Projects (repositories, default host and agent, the Unassigned project, making, editing and deleting them in Plan → Projects) and the rest of Plan as built, the Tasks board, starting a session from a task, Milestones, and the Calendar with Google Calendar.",
    body: "**Plan** is the rail item for organising work. Its sidebar has **Inbox**, **Tasks**, **Milestones**, **Calendar** and **Projects**. The Inbox has its own page: [Inbox](./inbox.md).\n\n## Projects\n\nA project is a saved scope with a name, zero or more repositories (each with a base branch), and defaults for new sessions: a host, an agent, and which repositories are cloned by default. Sessions, tasks, milestones and automations are filed under a project.\n\n- A project is metadata only. Moving a session to another project (**Move to project…** in the session's menu) changes nothing on the host: the folder, branch and running agent stay as they are.\n- A session that names no project is listed under **Unassigned**, a project every workspace has. It cannot be deleted or renamed away.\n- A project with no repositories is allowed: it holds folder sessions, terminal sessions, tasks and milestones.\n\n**Plan → Projects** is where projects are made and edited: it lists each project with its repositories, **New project** in the header, and each project's settings. New session's project chip and the Project facet of the Sessions sidebar's filter menu also end in **New project…**; editing stays in Plan. The dialog has the name, the repositories (picked from what the GitHub App can reach; \"Manage repository access\" adds more on GitHub), and the optional defaults.\n\n**Delete project** archives it: nothing new goes in it and the automations aimed at it are paused (they stay paused if it comes back). It is refused while the project has open sessions; move or delete them first. A deleted project's sessions and history stay.\n\n## The Tasks board\n\n**Plan → Tasks** is one board for the workspace, filtered by **Project** and **Milestone** at the top (each view remembers its filter on this device). Columns, in order: **Not started**, **In progress**, **In review**, **Done**. The header counts open, in progress, done and overdue tasks.\n\n- **New task** (or `n`) opens the task dialog: title, notes, Status, Project, Milestone, Sessions, TODO day (the day it goes on your TODO list, apart from when it is due) and Due (a date with Today, Tomorrow, Next Monday, and an optional time).\n- **Add task** at a column's foot adds a one-line task to that column under the current filter.\n- Drag cards within and across columns. Ticking a card's checkbox moves it to Done.\n- A card shows its title, notes, project, milestone, due date (red when overdue) and its session line.\n\nTasks have no assignee, since a workspace has one person. Tasks are Oppenheimer's own; there is no import from GitHub Issues or Linear.\n\n## TODO\n\n**Plan → TODO** is what you mean to work on, one day at a time (today unless you step to another day). A task's **TODO day** is separate from its **Due** date: a deadline does not have to be the day you work on it. For a day, the list shows:\n\n- the tasks put on that day and those due on it, timed ones first;\n- on today or a later day, open tasks put on an earlier day: they stay until they are done, so work that takes several days stays on the list (\"on TODO since …\");\n- with **Include overdue** on, open tasks due before today.\n\nA task ticked done stays on the day it was done. The dashed row at the foot adds a task put on that day with no due date, and **Add tasks** puts any open task on the day without changing its due date. Clicking a task opens its dialog over TODO; clear its TODO day there to take it off the list.\n\nA row's calendar button moves its TODO day: **Tomorrow**, or any day picked on the month it opens. A task due on the day shown, or overdue while Include overdue is on, has no such button: its deadline keeps it on the list whatever its TODO day, so change its due date in its dialog instead.\n\n**With agents**: a task on the list that an agent is working on (one of its sessions running or queued) moves to the **With agents** section under the list, with its session's state; it stays there even when it is due or overdue. Other tasks with sessions stay on the list with a mark saying why they need you, the most urgent first:\n\n- **waiting on you**: one of its sessions is waiting on your answer;\n- **failed**: one of its sessions failed;\n- **ready to review**: the task is in the board's In review column;\n- **agent stopped**: its sessions have stopped (idle or finished) and the task is still In progress.\n\nA session finishing does not finish the task or move it to In review; only you do that. The count in the header and beside TODO in the sidebar is what is left on today's list, not counting the tasks with agents.\n\n## Tasks and sessions\n\nA task is where work is asked for; a session is where it is done.\n\n- **Start session** (on a card with no session, or in the dialog's Sessions row) opens a dialog with a prompt prefilled from the title and notes, the **Repository** (the project's), **Where it runs** (on a host, Claude Code cloud from a host, or Cursor's cloud), the **Host**, and the **Agent and model**. The session starts at the Approve for me permission level. If the host is offline the button reads **Queue session** and the session starts when the host is back.\n- **Link existing** links sessions you already have; a session can be linked to several tasks.\n- Starting or linking a session moves a task that is in Not started to **In progress**. No other move is automatic.\n- The card's session line shows the linked session that most needs attention (Needs input first). Inside that session, a **Back to task** chip returns to the task.\n\nDeleting a task leaves its sessions as they are.\n\n## Milestones\n\nA milestone splits a project into stages with a target date (End of month and In 3 months are shortcuts). **Plan → Milestones** shows a project's milestones on a timeline, each with its progress: the share of its tasks that are done. A milestone without a target date has no place on the timeline but is still a filter on the board and a choice in the task dialog. Deleting a milestone keeps its tasks, without a milestone.\n\n## Calendar\n\n**Plan → Calendar** is a month view with four layers you can switch in the sidebar:\n\n| Layer | What it shows | Editable |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Personal | Events you create here (New event, or `n`): title, all day or times, notes | Yes; drag to another day |\n| Google Calendar | Events from connected Google accounts, each in its account's colour | No: read-only, change them in Google |\n| Task due dates | Tasks with a due date | Drag moves the due date |\n| Automations | When scheduled automations will run (hourly ones are left off) | No |\n\nThe header counts the month's meetings, tasks due and automation runs. Times are read in your browser's time zone.\n\n**Connecting Google Calendar**: use the Google Calendar card in the calendar's sidebar (**Connect Google Calendar**, then **Add account** for more, up to five). Nothing is written to Google. A Google event opens read-only, with **Open in Google Calendar**, and **Join meeting** when it is a Meet, Zoom, Teams or Webex meeting. Remove an account from the same card. There is no separate Settings page for it.\n\n## What bots can do here\n\nBots can list and read projects, tasks and milestones, create and edit tasks and move them between columns, create and edit projects, move sessions between projects, and archive (always asking first) or restore a project. They cannot read or edit the calendar. See [Bots](./bots.md).",
  },
  {
    id: "bots",
    title: "Bots (Employees)",
    description: "What a bot is and how to create one, its settings (Profile, Instructions with SOUL.md and INSTRUCTIONS.md, Memory, Skills, Permissions, Routines, Home host), model providers and keys, how a bot does code work through sessions, its tools, approvals, and its limits; its skills and commands are their own page.",
    body: "A **bot** is an LLM worker you chat with. The console calls the section **Employees**; inside it each one is a bot. A bot's loop runs inside Oppenheimer on the model provider you choose, not on a host. It has **no shell, no files and no access to the machine it runs on**. It acts through Oppenheimer's own tools, as you, and never with more access than you have. For work that needs a shell or a checkout, it starts a session on one of your hosts.\n\n## Creating a bot\n\nIn **Employees**, press **New bot**. A new bot gets a first name (Juno, Milo, Wren…), a colour and a drawn face: a round face in its colour wearing a hat, with eyes and a mouth taken from the bot itself, so it keeps its face through a rename. That first name is a placeholder: the bot is friendly and proactive by default, and in its first chat it asks what you want to call it (after any work your first message asks for) and saves your answer. It also tells you what it can do for you here, from the tools it has, and offers to help connect what is not connected yet. Its first chat also walks it through one job: tell it what it is for in a message, and it writes its own `INSTRUCTIONS.md` and description, remembers what it learned about you, and offers a first task. One bot per job works best.\n\nThe Employees sidebar holds your bots in groups (New group, Move to group) in an order you drag. A bot can also be shown in the Sessions, Automations, Plan and Pull requests sidebars: use the plus on a section's Bots heading, or **Shows in** on the bot's Profile. Its chat opens in place there; it is the same bot and the same conversation everywhere.\n\n## The chat\n\n- Each message starts a **turn**; one runs at a time per bot, and closing the tab does not stop it. **Stop** cancels it. While it runs, **Enter steers** it and **Alt+Enter queues** your message for after.\n- The chat shows the bot's text, an activity line per run of tool calls, cards for what needs you, and a line for every change it makes to itself.\n- You can send images (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP; up to 5 MB, four a message), except to GLM models. No other file types.\n- The composer picks the provider, model and effort, and its meter shows context use and, on a ChatGPT sign-in, the plan's limits.\n\n## Settings\n\nOpen a bot's settings from its menu. Every page saves with **Save**.\n\n| Tab | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| Profile | Name, description, colour, face (pick a hat, eyes and a mouth, or **Use its own face**), sidebar group, Shows in, and the chat history (Export, Clear history). |\n| Instructions | Two files read before every message: **SOUL.md** (personality and tone) and **INSTRUCTIONS.md** (its job and rules). Each save is a revision; Revision history can Revert. |\n| Memory | Facts it remembers, with who added each. **Learn automatically** lets it save facts without asking. A bot has two memories: its own, which only it reads and where everything about its job goes (your projects, repositories, folders, hosts, accounts, how you want that work done), and the **general memory** about you, which all your bots read and which only takes what a bot with another job would need too (your name, language, time zone, how you like to work everywhere). Every bot looks for something new about you in each conversation and adds it to the general memory, so all your bots get to know you better over time. A fact can also be kept in mind in every chat or looked up only when needed. Up to 500 facts. What every bot knows about you is also one page of its own, **Settings → Memory** at the account: an **About you** text you write (up to 4,000 characters), in every bot's prompt and never changed by a bot, and every fact about you your bots learned, each naming the bot that keeps it. About once a day, after it has saved enough and while Learn automatically is on, the bot **tidies its memory**: its own model merges repeats, drops what is stale or contradicted and rewrites each fact it wrote as one clear sentence, leaving the facts you wrote word for word. The chat shows a line (\"Tidied its memory · 40 facts → 22\"); **Tidy now** runs one whenever you like, and **Undo** puts the latest tidy back. |\n| Skills | Saved recipes for a task, at most 50. |\n| Permissions | Each tool set to Allow, Ask or Off; standing grants; plain-language rules. |\n| Routines | Its scheduled and event routines ([Routines and watches](./routines-and-watches.md)). |\n| Home host | Where its sessions start when nobody names a machine, or **No home host** (it asks each time). The bot itself always runs in Oppenheimer. |\n\n## Model providers\n\nKeys live in **Settings → Model providers**, one per provider for the workspace, stored encrypted and never shown again.\n\n| Provider | Models | How |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Anthropic | Claude | API key |\n| ChatGPT | GPT | **Connect ChatGPT**: sign in with a one-time code; runs on your ChatGPT subscription, not API billing |\n| OpenAI | GPT | API key |\n| xAI | Grok | API key |\n| GLM | GLM, through z.ai | API key |\n\n**Make default** picks the provider new bots start on. Without one, a bot uses the first provider that has a key, in the order Anthropic, ChatGPT, OpenAI, Grok, GLM. A bot whose provider has no key cannot answer until one is added.\n\n## How a bot works on code\n\nA bot answers what its own tools, web search and web fetch can answer. When the work needs a shell or a checkout (tests, a build, a fix) it starts a session: a coding agent on a host, in a known project's repository or a folder it was given, on the host you named, else the project's default, else its home host. Name a model, effort, permission level or base branch (\"on Opus 5.5 at high effort, from develop\") and it uses them; the rest is the CLI's default, at Approve for me. Full access is approved session by session. It follows that session, is woken when the agent needs input, finishes, fails or looks stuck, looks in every few minutes while the agent works, and reports what the session shows. It follows a session it prompted or unblocked the same way, and any other it says it will come back to (`watch_session`), at a time it names if it said one.\n\nIt can also prompt a session whose agent waits at its prompt, answer an agent's question or permission dialog, stop a session, close a session a bot started, such as one that failed (`close_session`), read a session's log and conversation, and start cloud sessions. When a session it follows stops on a question or a permission, the bot answers it itself so the work carries on, rather than sending you to the session; it always says why it answered as it did. On **Full access** it decides even a dialog that would let something destructive run (a recursive delete, a forced push, `sudo`); on **Approve for me** it asks you first, and such a dialog gets a card of its own, whatever you allowed before. To find a session, it can search every session of your workspace, on any host and closed ones included, for words in its name and log: the tasks and prompts it was given, the agent's results, notes, questions and failures (`search_sessions`). It does not search the agents' own conversations, which stay on the host.\n\n## What else it can do\n\n- **Itself**: edit SOUL.md, INSTRUCTIONS.md, name and description; remember, forget, recall; search its chat.\n- **Hosts**: list your hosts with their runner version, and update a host's runner (`update_host_runner`, the same as **Update runner** in the host's menu; it asks first under Approve for me, and only reaches an online host). When a session's conversation, a shared file or a terminal watch needs a newer runner, the bot can offer to update it.\n- **Plan**: read projects and tasks; create, edit and move tasks; create and edit projects; move sessions; archive or restore a project.\n- **GitHub**: read repositories, pull requests and issues; comment, review, merge, start review sessions, open issues, record CI findings, all in your name.\n- **Automations**: list them and their runs. It cannot create or change yours; it manages only its own routines.\n- **Other bots**: message one, ask one a task (the answer comes back as a new turn), create one. Chains stop at 3 bots deep and 10 bot-to-bot turns.\n- **Mail, WhatsApp and Telegram**: with Gmail connected in **Settings → Mail**, search, read, draft, send, schedule, label, archive and trash mail. With a number linked in **Settings → WhatsApp** (held by a host; use a spare number), read chats and send messages. With a bot of yours from @BotFather connected in **Settings → Telegram** (its token stays on the host), read the chats people have with it and answer them; Telegram lets a bot write only to chats that wrote to it first.\n- **Work**: web search, fetch public pages, show images from the web, run up to four read-only subagents, ask you up to four questions on a card, `notify` your Inbox, and read this guide (`read_oppenheimer_docs`).\n\n## Skills and slash commands\n\nA bot keeps **skills**, saved ways of doing one task, which you run by typing `/` and a name; it also has built-in skills (`oppenheimer`, `/recap`) and built-in commands (`/goal`, `/usage`, `/loop`, `/schedule`). See [Bot skills and commands](./bot-skills.md).\n\n## Permissions and approvals\n\nThe composer's **approval level** is a preset: **Approve for me** (what stays inside Oppenheimer runs; what reaches outside asks: starting or prompting sessions, GitHub writes, creating bots, sends) or **Full access** (everything runs without asking, except the few calls below that always ask). **Settings → Permissions** overrides any tool with **Allow**, **Ask** or **Off** (Off hides it from the bot).\n\nWhen a tool asks, the turn ends on a **Needs input** card in the chat and in the Inbox. The card offers what fits the call: **Approve once**, **Always allow for** the repository, project, session or chat it acts on, **Always allow**, **Deny** (the bot is told and carries on), or **Deny and stop**. Writing to the bot instead dismisses the card. Some calls always ask, under Full access too: sending, scheduling or trashing mail, and archiving a project. Merging a pull request is approved one call at a time (no **Always allow**, and setting it to Allow does not stop it asking under Approve for me), but under **Full access** a bot merges without asking unless you set merging to **Ask** in **Settings → Permissions**. Rules (\"Ask me first when…\", \"Go ahead without asking when…\") are checked before an asking call; an ask rule wins.\n\n## Limits\n\n- A turn has no step or time limit: it runs until the bot answers. When it has read about 90,000 tokens it summarises what came before and carries on, and the chat shows a line where it did. It stops if the model sends nothing for 5 minutes while no tool runs, or if it keeps repeating calls that change nothing. Older history is summarised for the model; the chat keeps every message.\n- A bot cannot pair or remove hosts, connect GitHub, Google or Gmail, share sessions, manage API tokens, or delete bots.\n- Not available: calendar tools, files other than images, group chats, bot templates, and writing to a bot from Slack, mail, WhatsApp or Telegram.",
  },
  {
    id: "bot-skills",
    title: "Bot skills and commands",
    description: "A bot's skills (saved ways of doing a task, run as /<name>, saved by you or by the bot, which also fixes them with patch_skill), the built-in skills oppenheimer and /recap, and the built-in commands /goal /usage /loop /schedule.",
    body: "A **skill** is a saved way of doing one task: when it applies, the steps, how to check, what to report. Ask the bot to \"save that as a skill\", or add one in **Settings → Skills**. Run it by typing `/` and its name in the chat; the bot can also use a skill on its own when a request fits, if you allow that on the skill.\n\n## Skills the bot saves and fixes\n\nA bot also keeps its skills up to date on its own. After a task that took real work and is likely to come again, it may save how it did it as a skill without being asked, at most one a turn, never for a one-off or anything private, and it says so by name. When a step of a skill it followed turns out wrong or out of date, it fixes that step with `patch_skill` once the task is done and tells you. It changes a skill you wrote only when you ask it to; otherwise it proposes the change in its answer. Each save or fix shows as a line in the chat, and the skill is in **Settings → Skills**, marked as the bot's.\n\n## Built-in skills\n\nEvery bot also has one built-in skill, `oppenheimer`: before it answers a question about Oppenheimer itself, how to set something up in it, or what bots can do, it reads this guide with `read_oppenheimer_docs`, answers from the pages, and says so when the guide does not cover it. You do not run it; ask your question and the bot reads. No skill of yours can take that name.\n\nA second built-in skill, `recap`, catches you up: type `/recap`, or `/recap` and a topic, or just ask \"where are we?\", and the bot reads your sessions, your pull requests, your plan and this chat, then answers with where things stand, what is waiting on you and the next move. It only reads. When a session the bot follows finishes, its report ends with a recap of that session, unasked. A Claude Code session does the same when you ask it to recall or catch you up, and ends each task it finishes with one; a Codex session does not yet.\n\n## Built-in commands\n\nBuilt-in commands work for every bot:\n\n| Command | Does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `/goal <condition>` | Keep working across turns, with no limit, until the condition holds. When the bot says it is met, a reviewer on the bot's model checks what it showed. The goal pauses, and keeps its place, when a turn of it fails or is stopped (a provider's usage limit whose end is known is waited out, and the goal goes on by itself), after 3 turns that call no tool, when it spends its token budget (if the bot set one) or when the bot says it needs you; **Resume** above the composer or `/goal resume` runs it again. Stop or `/goal clear` ends it. |\n| `/usage` | A card with this chat's turns, tokens, cache hits, and plan limits where reported. No prices. |\n| `/loop [interval] [prompt]` | Run a prompt now and every 1 minute to 1 day, or when the bot chooses. One per bot; ends after 3 days, on `/loop stop`, or when the bot ends it. |\n| `/schedule <request>` | Save a routine from your words (what and when). `/schedule` alone opens Routines. |\n\n`/plan` and `/grill-me` are planned, not available.",
  },
  {
    id: "routines-and-watches",
    title: "Routines and watches",
    description: "How a bot works when nobody writes to it, routines on a schedule or on a GitHub or Gmail event, its hourly heartbeat, quiet runs, loops and goals, watches on sessions and pull requests, the stall check, the regular look at work in progress, and the full list of what wakes a bot.",
    body: "A bot only runs when something gives it a **turn**. Your message is one. This page covers the others: routines, loops, goals and watches. Every one of them is a queued turn of the bot, runs as you with the bot's permissions as they are at that moment, and shows in the chat as a line saying why it ran. A routine's run is the exception: it has no line, so the bot's message reads as the bot coming back to you on its own.\n\n## Routines\n\nA **routine** is an automation whose run is a turn of the bot instead of a session. It lasts until deleted and runs whether or not the chat is open. Routines are listed on the bot's **Routines** tab and on **Automations** with the bot that runs them.\n\nTriggers a routine can have:\n\n| Trigger | Examples |\n| --- | --- |\n| Schedule | Once on a date, hourly, daily, weekdays, weekly on chosen days, monthly, in your time zone |\n| GitHub event | Pull request opened, pushed or merged, check failed, checks passed, review submitted, comment, issue opened, label added, mention, push, release (on repositories the GitHub App covers) |\n| Gmail | Mail received in a connected Gmail account, optionally matching a search |\n\n**Making one**: ask the bot in chat (\"every weekday at 9, check open PRs and tell me which need me\"), or type `/schedule` followed by the request. The bot saves it with its routine tool. A save asks you first with a card, except a create from your own `/schedule` command, which is the consent. Changing, deleting or running a routine from the chat asks too.\n\n**The Routines tab** is controls, not an editor. Each row shows the name, when it runs (or Paused) and its last and next run, with a switch, **Run now**, **Edit in chat** (puts \"Edit your routine: …\" in the composer for the bot to change it), and **Delete**. Using the tab yourself raises no card.\n\nA routine is skipped and paused when it cannot run, with the reason in the chat: the bot's provider has no key, its owner lost access, or an hourly run limit was hit (10 runs an hour per routine, 100 per workspace by default). A run due while the last one is still running is skipped.\n\n**The heartbeat.** Once a bot has a job (its `INSTRUCTIONS.md` is written), it is given one routine of its own, **Heartbeat**: hourly, at a minute past the hour that differs from bot to bot, so your bots do not all wake at once. Each run the bot rereads its instructions and the plan, looks at the sessions, pull requests and mail it looks after, acts on what needs it and tells you what you must know; when nothing does, it stays quiet. It is an ordinary routine: pause, edit or delete it on the Routines tab or in chat. A bot that already had a job before heartbeats existed has none until you ask for one.\n\n**Quiet runs.** A turn nobody is waiting on (a routine, a wake, a goal, a loop) can end with nothing to report: the chat shows only a collapsed line, and you get no message. A routine's quiet run shows nothing at all, unless the chat is set to show tool calls.\n\n**While you're away.** Routines keep running on their schedule whether or not you read what they say; nothing pauses them because you have not opened the chat. To stop one, pause it on the Routines tab or ask the bot.\n\nMail older than two days, spam, trash and chats never wake a Gmail routine; email and event text reach the bot marked as outside data, never as instructions.\n\n## Loops\n\n`/loop` is for watching something now, in this chat: `/loop 5m check the deploy` runs the prompt at once and then every 5 minutes (1 minute to 1 day). Without an interval the bot picks when to run next, 1 to 60 minutes ahead. A bot has one loop at a time. It ends after 3 days, when you press Stop or type `/loop stop`, when the bot decides it has served its purpose, or when a run fails in a way the next would too. Use `/schedule` for a habit, `/loop` for minutes.\n\n## Goals\n\nA goal keeps a bot going across turns until a condition holds: you type `/goal <condition>`, or the bot sets one itself. After each turn a reviewer on the bot's model judges it met, not met or impossible without you; not met queues the next continuation. Your goals get up to 20 continuations, the bot's 5 unless it asks for more. Stop above the composer or `/goal clear` ends it. A goal waits out a provider's rate limit and says until when.\n\n## Watches\n\nA **watch** (a subscription) makes a bot hear back from work it does not own the clock of.\n\n**Sessions.** When a bot starts a session it follows it by default. It can also watch any session (`watch_session`): until the current task ends, or until the session closes. A watched session wakes the bot when its task:\n\n| Event | The bot is asked to |\n| --- | --- |\n| Needs input (a permission, a question) | tell you what it waits on and where |\n| Completes | read what it did and report it |\n| Fails | say so and why |\n| Is stopped or closed | say it was stopped, without restarting it |\n| Expires before running | say so |\n\nA watch can also wake on a line the terminal prints that matches a pattern; the match is made on the host and only a count reaches Oppenheimer, so the bot reads the session to see what happened. A notice the session's agent sends with `runner sessions notify` wakes every bot that follows it. Each wake fires once per state per task. A watch ends after 20 wakes; a bot holds at most 50 watches.\n\n**The stall check.** Five minutes after a bot starts a session or sends one a prompt, Oppenheimer checks once whether the task never started or its agent was never seen working (an offline host, an agent stuck at a sign-in). If so the bot is woken once to tell you what looks stuck. It does not restart or prompt the session on its own.\n\n**Looking at the work.** While that task keeps running, the bot looks at it again every ten minutes, for up to an hour: it reads what the agent has done against the task it gave. When the work is on track it says nothing, and the chat shows only \"Checked on session …\". When the agent has gone idle without finishing, is going in circles or off the task, waits on something or hits an error, the bot tells you, and it may nudge the agent when its instructions cover what the agent needs. Ask it \"how is it going?\" and it looks right away.\n\n**Waiting inside a turn.** A bot can also wait up to 120 seconds inside one turn for a session to need input or end, at most three times a turn.\n\n**Pull requests.** `watch_pull_request` follows an open pull request on a repository you can read. A check failing or passing, a review, a comment or the merge wakes the bot with the event's facts (kind, number, check name, reviewer's login), never anyone's words; the bot reads the pull request itself. The merge ends the watch. A pull request closed without merging does not end it yet.\n\nAsk a bot \"what are you watching?\" and it lists its watches. There is no list of them in the bot's settings yet; every wake is a line in the chat.\n\n## Everything that wakes a bot\n\n| Source | Turn opened by |\n| --- | --- |\n| Your message, or a decision on its approval or question card | you |\n| Another bot's message or ask, or the answer to its own ask | that bot |\n| A routine's schedule, GitHub event or Gmail mail, or Run now | the routine |\n| A loop's run, a goal's continuation | the loop or goal |\n| A watched session or pull request, a session's notice, the stall check, the regular look at a running task | the watch |\n\nTurns run one at a time per bot: your messages first, then other bots', then background work (routines, loops, wakes).\n\n## Not available yet\n\nSpreading the run times of routines other than the heartbeat, routines triggered by a session ending or by a webhook, and WhatsApp or Telegram messages as a trigger.",
  },
  {
    id: "automations",
    title: "Automations",
    description: "Automations as built, a saved prompt that starts a session on a schedule, a GitHub event or Run now, how to create one with the three-step editor, where each run starts, the limits and guards, why a run is skipped or an automation paused, and what is not built yet.",
    body: "An **automation** is a saved prompt with a project, repositories, a place to run, a host, an agent and model, and one or more triggers. Any trigger starts a **run**, and each run is an ordinary session started as you, with the prompt as its first task. You watch it, type into it and delete it like any other session.\n\nBot routines are automations too, whose run is a turn of a bot instead of a session; they are listed here beside yours ([Routines and watches](./routines-and-watches.md)).\n\n## Where it is\n\nThe **Automations** rail item:\n\n- The sidebar lists automations by project, with New automation, a search, and per row a clock or GitHub mark and its status (Running, Paused, the time to the next run, or its run count). Selecting one shows its last six runs.\n- **Automations** tab: a 30-day run history chart and the table (trigger, next run, status, and a row menu with Edit, Run now, Pause or Resume, Duplicate, Delete).\n- **Runs** tab: every run, filtered by status (All, Completed, Failed, Running), automation, project and time window.\n- **One automation**: Run now, Edit, a menu with pause, duplicate and delete, its facts line, its run history and its runs.\n- **A run** opens the session it started, with the automations list kept beside it.\n\n## Creating one\n\n**New automation** opens an editor with three steps:\n\n1. **Task**: a name, and what the agent should do.\n2. **Trigger**: add one or more. A **schedule** (once, hourly, daily, weekdays, weekly on chosen days, monthly, at a time in your zone), or a **GitHub event** on chosen repositories, with one filter value or any. A GitHub trigger shows how many times it would have run on recent events.\n3. **Where it runs**: the project and repositories, where the runs go (on a host, Claude Code's cloud from a host, or Cursor's cloud), the host, and the agent and model. These start from the project's defaults.\n\nCreate is on the last step.\n\n## GitHub events\n\n| Event | The run starts on |\n| --- | --- |\n| Pull request opened, Draft opened, Pull request pushed, Pull request merged, Check failed, Checks passed, Review submitted | the pull request's head branch |\n| Push to branch | the pushed branch |\n| Comment added, Label added, Issue opened, Mentioned @oppenheimer, Release published, a schedule, Run now | a fresh worktree on the default branch |\n\nThe event's details reach the agent after the prompt, marked as outside data it should not take instructions from. Events caused by Oppenheimer's own GitHub App never trigger a run. A run may push to any branch its token reaches; there are no push guardrails yet.\n\n## Pausing and Run now\n\nA paused automation ignores its triggers; **Run now** still works. Oppenheimer pauses an automation itself, with the reason in a band on its page, when:\n\n- its project was deleted (move it to another project to resume);\n- its host was removed (pick another host in Edit);\n- its owner can no longer run it;\n- its cloud provider has no key or refused it (connect it in **Settings → Cloud agents**).\n\n## Limits and guards\n\n| Limit | Default |\n| --- | --- |\n| Runs per automation per hour | 10 |\n| Runs per workspace per hour | 100 |\n| Live automation runs per host | 2 (your own sessions never count) |\n| A run still going when the next fires | skipped |\n| A run left pending (host offline, host full, under 5 GB free disk) | expires after 1 hour |\n| A scheduled slot missed while it could not run | skipped after 15 minutes |\n| Maximum run duration | 1 hour, then its session is stopped |\n\nA skipped run is still listed, with its reason: paused, deleted, own event, hourly limit, still running, missed slot, could not start, or agent not on the host.\n\nDeleting an automation stops its triggers and keeps its runs, which read \"Deleted automation\".\n\n## From elsewhere\n\n- `opp automations list | create | run | pause | resume | delete` and the MCP server's automation tools ([CLI and MCP](./cli-and-mcp.md)).\n- Bots can list automations and read their run history, but cannot create, change or run your automations.\n- Plan's calendar shows when scheduled automations will run (hourly ones are left off).\n- A failed run in the last day shows in the [Inbox](./inbox.md) with **Run again**.\n\n## Not built yet\n\n- Templates.\n- Headless runs: every run is an interactive session in a terminal; there is no structured result beside it.\n- Resuming or continuing a finished run from its page, and keeping its worktree after the run.\n- Slack and Linear triggers, push guardrails, and handing an automation to someone else.",
  },
  {
    id: "inbox",
    title: "Inbox",
    description: "The Inbox in Plan, what lands there (waiting or failed sessions, bot approvals, review requests, mergeable pull requests, failed automation runs, notices from sessions and bots, reports with pictures from any agent, tasks an agent made), what you can do with each item, filters, unread, snooze and done, and how agents and bots post to it.",
    body: "The **Inbox** is one list of what needs you. It is the first item of **Plan** (Plan → Inbox), and the console opens on it unless you dragged another rail item to the top. Plan's sidebar shows its count.\n\nIt looks like a mail client: a list column (search, unread rows with the sender, a subject and a line of body) beside a reading pane with a toolbar and the item as a message. On a phone the list fills the screen until you open an item.\n\n## What lands there\n\nItems are not stored, with one exception: an item is there while its source says it needs you, and leaves when it no longer does. The exception is a **report**, which an agent sends and Oppenheimer keeps until you answer it.\n\n| Item | From | When | What you can do |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A session waiting on you | Sessions | its agent has been blocked 30 seconds or more, it failed to start, or it has been starting for over a minute | **Open session**. An agent's permission prompt or multiple-choice question can be answered right in the Inbox (**Send answers**); your answer is typed into its terminal |\n| A bot approval | Bots | a bot stopped on a tool set to ask | **Approve**, **Deny**, or **Open chat** |\n| A review request (when shown) | Pull requests | someone asked for your review | **Open**, **View on GitHub** |\n| A pull request ready to merge (when shown) | Pull requests | your pull request has nothing holding it | **Merge**, **Open**, **View on GitHub** |\n| A failed automation run | Automations | a run failed in the last 24 hours | **Run again**, **Open run** |\n| A session's notice | an agent in a session | the agent ran `runner sessions notify \"…\"` | **Done** |\n| A bot's notice | a bot | the bot used its `notify` tool | **Open chat**, **Done** |\n| A report | any agent with a token | it posted a problem, with up to four pictures | Write an answer and **Send answer**; the agent reads it. **Done** hides it without answering |\n| A task an agent made | a bot, or an agent with a token (MCP, CLI) | it added a task to your board, and you have not taken it up | **Put on today**, **Start session**, **Dismiss** (keeps it on the board as it is), **Open task** |\n\nPull requests are off by default: agents open theirs under your GitHub account, so every one would wait here as yours to merge. Turn on **Show pull requests** in the filter menu to list review requests and ready merges; the choice is kept in this browser. The **Pull requests** page lists them either way.\n\nA task an agent made leaves the Inbox once you take it up: you give it a TODO day (here or in its dialog), start or link a session on it, or dismiss it. **Delete** on one dismisses it too; **Mark as done** only hides it for you, as for any item: it stays under Show done for your visit and the task still waits to be taken up. Only you take a task up: a session or a TODO day an agent gives it leaves it waiting. A task you make in the console never lands here. Tasks made through the CLI or an API token count as an agent's, even when you typed the command.\n\nA session or bot keeps one notice at a time: a newer one replaces the last. Questions a bot asks you with a question card are answered in its chat, not in the Inbox.\n\nThe list is newest first across every kind; deprioritized items sort last and read **Low**. Every item carries the project it belongs to, for the project filter.\n\n## Acting on items\n\nThe reading pane's toolbar and each row's menu:\n\n| Action | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Mark as done** | Hides this occurrence. It shows under **Show done** for the rest of your visit. |\n| **Deprioritize** / **Restore priority** | Sorts it last, labelled Low, or puts it back. |\n| **Snooze 1 hour** / **Snooze until tomorrow** | Hides it until then, in **Snoozed** with **Bring back**. |\n| **Delete** | Hides this occurrence for good. |\n\nA mark applies to one occurrence: if the same session blocks again later, or new commits land on a pull request, the item comes back.\n\n**Unread** is kept per item in this browser: an item is read once shown in the reading pane. It does not sync between devices.\n\n**Filters**: Type (All, Pull requests while they are shown, Bots, Sessions, Automations, Reports, Tasks), Project, Show done, Show pull requests, and a search over sender, subject, body and source.\n\nWhen nothing needs you, the pane says so and counts the sessions still running.\n\n## Posting to the Inbox\n\n- **An agent in a session** runs `runner sessions notify \"The migration ran; all tests pass\"` (one line, at most 500 bytes). Use it when the agent finishes something you asked to hear about, or needs you while you may not be watching. A bot that follows that session is woken by the notice too.\n- **A bot** calls its `notify` tool: one line, at most 500 characters, for a bot whose chat nobody is watching (a routine's result, a long goal).\n\n- **Any agent with a token** sends a **report**: a problem with its pictures, which you answer in the Inbox. This is the way for an agent that is not in a session on one of your hosts, such as a scheduled routine in a vendor's cloud.\n\n## Reports: a problem with pictures, and your answer\n\nA report has a title, an optional body, the sender's name for itself, an optional link to where the problem can be seen, and up to four pictures (PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP, 5 MB each). The agent needs an [API token](./cli-and-mcp.md) with the **Inbox reports** permissions (`inbox-reports:write` to send, `inbox-reports:read` to read the answer), pinned to your workspace as `opp tokens create` pins it; a token pinned to no workspace is refused with `TENANT_001`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -sS -X POST \"$OPPENHEIMER_API_URL/v1/inbox/reports\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $OPPENHEIMER_API_TOKEN\" \\\n  -F title=\"Sign-up shows a blank page on a phone\" \\\n  -F body=\"Opened /sign-up at 390 px wide; the form never renders. Should I fix it or is this known?\" \\\n  -F source=\"qa-explorer\" \\\n  -F link=\"https://github.com/acme/app/pull/123\" \\\n  -F images=@before.png -F images=@console-error.png\n```\n\nThe answer comes back on the report itself. The agent asks for it when it next runs:\n\n```bash\ncurl -sS \"$OPPENHEIMER_API_URL/v1/inbox/reports/<id>\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $OPPENHEIMER_API_TOKEN\"\n# \"status\": \"answered\", \"answer\": \"Fix it, and add a test at phone width.\"\n```\n\n`GET /v1/inbox/reports?status=answered` lists every answered report, and `?status=open` those still waiting on you.\n\nIn the Inbox a report shows its pictures (select one to see it whole), its link, and a box for your answer. Once you send an answer the report leaves the Inbox; nothing is typed into a terminal and nobody is woken, so an agent that runs once a day sees your answer on its next run. A token that holds only the Inbox reports permissions cannot answer a report: answering takes the **Inbox** permission, which is yours.\n\n## Notifications elsewhere\n\nThere are no push notifications or emails for Inbox items. Oppenheimer emails you only for account and security events, such as a newly paired host or a host connecting from a new country or network. The Oppenheimer Chrome extension tracks your Claude Code cloud sessions and tells you which need you.",
  },
  {
    id: "github",
    title: "GitHub and pull requests",
    description: "Connecting GitHub by installing the Oppenheimer GitHub App, which repositories sessions can reach and how git access works in a session, and the Pull requests section as built (PR queue, the review deck, lanes, filters, merging, Description, Changes, review sessions, Analytics, Watched repositories).",
    body: "Oppenheimer reaches your code through the **Oppenheimer GitHub App**. Signing in with GitHub and connecting GitHub are separate: you can sign in with Google or email and still connect GitHub, and the App installation, not your sign-in, decides which repositories Oppenheimer can see.\n\n## Connecting GitHub\n\n- During first-run, **Connect GitHub** installs the App on your account or on an organization. On GitHub's own page you choose **all repositories** or **selected** ones.\n- Later, every repository picker (New session's Repositories chip, a project's repositories) ends in **Manage repository access**, which opens the App's installation page on GitHub to add or remove repositories or install it on another account.\n- For an organization you do not own, the install becomes a request: an owner of the organization approves it on GitHub, then you choose Connect GitHub again and pick that organization.\n\nOnly repositories an installation covers appear in the pickers. Archived repositories are marked.\n\n## Git in a session\n\n- A worktree session clones the repository onto a branch of its own, `oppenheimer/<slug>`, cut from the base branch you picked.\n- Git authenticates through a credential helper the runner sets up for that session: it hands git a **one-hour token scoped to the session's repositories**, minted by Oppenheimer when needed. No GitHub token is written to the host's disk. Separately, when the deployment has a key for it (`GITHUB_USER_TOKEN_KEY`), connecting GitHub keeps your own GitHub user token, encrypted in Oppenheimer's database, so the Pull requests section can comment, review and merge as you; it is refreshed as needed and dropped when it can no longer be opened or refreshed, and you are asked to connect again.\n- Deleting a session pushes its branch if it has commits, then removes the worktree.\n- There is no \"Create pull request\" button. The agent opens pull requests itself, for example with `gh pr create` (install `gh` on the host), and can then run `runner sessions watch-pr <number>` to be woken when its checks finish, it is reviewed or commented on, or it merges.\n- A folder session uses your own git credentials on the host, not the App.\n\n## Pull requests\n\nThe **Pull requests** rail item has three views in its sidebar, **PR queue**, **Review** and **Analytics**, and **For review** listing your review sessions by repository.\n\n### The PR queue\n\nThe queue reads the open pull requests of the repositories you watch.\n\n- **Whose**: **All**, **Mine** (opened by you or your sessions), **Review requests** (someone asked for your review), **Watching** (the rest).\n- **Lanes**: each pull request is placed in **Deep**, **Medium** or **Quick** by its difficulty. The order is fixed: Deep first, then Medium, then Quick, the longest wait first within a lane.\n- **Filters**: a repository picker, a search over title, repository, number and author, and facets for Difficulty, Checks (passing, failing, running) and Waiting (under 1 hour, 1 hour to 1 day, over 1 day). Filters live in the address.\n- Each row shows the lane, title, size, checks, conflicts and how long it has waited, with **Review** (opens the changes) and **Merge** (squash and merge into the base, after a confirm).\n- Keys: `j` and `k` move, Enter opens, `/` searches, `N` starts the review deck on the next one.\n\n**Settings → Watched repositories** picks the repositories whose pull requests fill the Watching tab, even when nobody asked for your review.\n\n### Review: one pull request at a time\n\n**Review** walks the queue as a deck of cards, in the queue's order, with the scope and filters it was opened from. **Review next** in the queue opens it.\n\n- Each card shows the pull request's title, author and lane, a **brief** (one sentence of what it changes, the pictures from its description, a few points and where to look first), and its checks, conflicts, size and wait.\n- The brief is written by the deployment's language model from the description and the diff, once per head commit. Where no model is configured it is taken from the author's description instead. The card says which.\n- Pictures the description links to are shown when your browser can load them; pictures attached to a private repository's pull request usually cannot be, and are left out.\n- Actions, each of which moves to the next card: **Pass** (`←`); on your own pull requests **Merge** (`→`, press twice) and **Comment** (`C`); on others' **Approve** (`→`), **Approve and merge** (`M`, press twice) and **Request changes** (`C`). A held key does nothing after its first press.\n- A card stays at the high level and never shows the diff: **Review in depth** on the card (Enter) opens the pull request's changes, and Back returns to the same card.\n- After the last card you can walk them again or go back to the queue.\n\n### One pull request\n\n- **Description**: the header (state, lane, number, title, author, head and base branches), the author's description with Edit on GitHub, and a rail with merge status, reviews, comments and checks. Where a bot recorded CI findings, each failing check shows its cause, whether it is flaky and the suggested action.\n- **Changes**: the diff, unified or split, with a file list, Viewed marks and line comments. Comments are pending until you submit a review.\n- **Submit review**: **Comment**, **Approve**, **Approve and merge** (merges now when GitHub allows, otherwise turns on GitHub's auto-merge so it merges once required checks pass), or **Request changes**. Reviews and merges are made as you on GitHub, and GitHub's branch rules still apply; if GitHub will not allow it, the pull request waits.\n- **Chat**: **Start review session** starts Claude Code on one of your online hosts (the chip picks which), in a worktree of the pull request's head branch. It reads the diff, opens with two or three lines (what it changes, the riskiest part, where to look first), then waits for your questions; it asks before every edit and command. Review sessions are listed under For review, never under Sessions. Pull requests from forks cannot be checked out yet.\n\n### Analytics\n\nYour review over a week, month or quarter on watched repositories: pull requests created and merged per day, how many you reviewed, the median wait for review and time to merge, agent sessions against people, the lane mix, and what holds open pull requests now.\n\n## GitHub in the rest of Oppenheimer\n\n- **Automations** start on GitHub events such as a pull request opened, a check failed or a mention ([Automations](./automations.md)).\n- **Bots** list repositories and pull requests, read them, comment, review, merge (asking first under Approve for me, one call at a time and pinned to the commit the card showed; under Full access it merges without asking unless you set merging to Ask in Settings → Permissions), start review sessions, read and open issues, and follow a pull request with a watch ([Bots](./bots.md), [Routines and watches](./routines-and-watches.md)). Issue tools need the App's Issues permission accepted on the installation.\n- The **Inbox** lists review requests and your pull requests that are ready to merge.\n\n## Not available yet\n\nA generated summary of a pull request, a merge confidence score, per-repository policies on the Watched repositories page, notifications from GitHub, checking out pull requests from forks, and push guardrails for sessions and automations.",
  },
  {
    id: "cli-and-mcp",
    title: "CLI and MCP",
    description: "Driving Oppenheimer from scripts and other agents with the opp command line and the MCP server, signing in, scoped API tokens and their scopes, handing work to another host, the commands and tools available, exit codes, and current limits.",
    body: "Two clients drive the same API the console uses, on **scoped API tokens**:\n\n- **`opp`**, a command line for people, scripts and CI, and for an agent in a session that wants to hand work to an agent on **another host**.\n- **The Oppenheimer MCP server**, which gives an MCP client (Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor) tools for your hosts, sessions, automations, bots and tasks.\n\nOn the same host, an agent in a session does not need either: `runner sessions run` starts a child session there without a token ([Sessions](./sessions.md)).\n\n## Getting `opp`\n\n`opp` is built from the Oppenheimer repository (`apps/cli`); it is not yet shipped with the runner or as a package, so a host that should run it needs it installed and a token given to it.\n\n## Signing in and tokens\n\n```bash\nopp login                                     # email and password, traded at once for a scoped token\nopp login --permissions hosts:read,sessions:write   # a narrower token\nopp login --with-token oppenheimer_pat_…      # a token you already have\nopp whoami                                    # who you are and what the token can do\nopp logout                                    # revokes the token\n```\n\n`opp login` never stores your session; it stores only the token, in `~/.config/oppenheimer/config.json` (mode 0600). Profiles let one machine talk to several deployments (`opp --profile staging login`). `OPPENHEIMER_API_URL` and `OPPENHEIMER_API_TOKEN` override the profile.\n\nTokens are managed with `opp tokens list | create | revoke | permissions`. A token:\n\n- carries only the scopes it was given, never more than you hold (`opp tokens permissions` shows what you may grant);\n- acts inside a workspace only when it is pinned to one; `opp login` and `opp tokens create` pin it to yours;\n- is checked against your current roles on every call.\n\nScopes are `resource:read` or `resource:write` (write includes read), for resources such as `hosts`, `projects`, `sessions`, `automations`, `bots`, `tasks`, `repositories`, `pulls` and `inbox`.\n\n| Command | Needs |\n| --- | --- |\n| `sessions run` | `sessions:write`, `hosts:read`, `projects:read`, and `repositories:read` for a repository no project holds |\n| `sessions transcript` | `sessions:read` |\n| `sessions send`, `stop`, `close` | `sessions:write` |\n| `automations create` | `automations:write`, `hosts:read`, `projects:read` |\n| `bots chat` | `bots:write` |\n| `tasks add`, `move` | `tasks:write` |\n\nThe console has no page for API tokens; use `opp tokens`.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | Does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `opp hosts list \\| get` | Your hosts and which are online |\n| `opp projects list` | Projects with their repositories, default host and agent |\n| `opp sessions list \\| get \\| run \\| send \\| wait \\| stop \\| close` | Sessions on your hosts |\n| `opp sessions run --hidden`, `opp sessions hide \\| show <id>` | Keep a session out of the console's sidebar, or show it again; it runs the same |\n| `opp sessions transcript` | A session's conversation, a page at a time (`--cursor` reads on), secrets replaced |\n| `opp automations list \\| create \\| run \\| pause \\| resume \\| delete` | Automations |\n| `opp bots list \\| chat` | Your bots; `chat` waits for the answer |\n| `opp tasks list \\| add \\| move` | Plan's board |\n| `opp mcp install \\| status` | Connect an MCP client to this deployment |\n\nEvery command takes `--json` and prints the API's answer unchanged. Names (a host, a project, `owner/name`, a bot) are resolved, and refused when ambiguous.\n\n## Handing work to another host\n\n```bash\nopp hosts list\nopp sessions run --host mac-studio --repo acme/app \"Run the iOS tests; report what fails\"\nopp sessions wait <id>\nopp sessions get <id>\nopp sessions send <id> \"Fix the first failure and push\"\nopp sessions close <id>\n```\n\n- `--repo owner/name` is looked up in your projects, then in any installation that grants it; `--project` uses the project's default repository and host; `--folder /path` runs in a folder already on the host.\n- `send` works only while the agent waits at its prompt; the session's log records the prompt as yours.\n- `wait` returns when the agent is idle, ready for review, waiting on you, or gone. After `--timeout` (default 30 minutes) it exits 1 and the session keeps running. `run --wait` and `send --wait` wait for the agent to take the prompt first.\n- A session started this way is an ordinary session in the console.\n\nExit codes: 0 success, 1 failure (a timed-out wait included), 2 bad usage, 3 not signed in, 4 not permitted, 5 not found, 6 API unreachable.\n\n## The MCP server\n\n```bash\nopp login\nopp tokens create --name \"Claude\" --permissions hosts:read,projects:read,sessions:write,bots:write\nopp --token <the token> mcp install --client claude-code\nopp mcp status\n```\n\nThe server runs over stdio with an API token, or as one hosted server over Streamable HTTP with a bearer token per request. An agent sees only the tools its token's scopes allow.\n\n| Area | Tools |\n| --- | --- |\n| Who | `whoami` (call it first when a tool is missing) |\n| Hosts and projects | `list_hosts`, `list_projects` |\n| Sessions | `list_sessions`, `get_session`, `read_session_transcript`, `wait_for_session`, `start_session`, `send_to_session`, `set_session_visibility`, `stop_session`, `close_session` |\n| Automations | `list_automations`, `create_automation`, `run_automation`, `pause_automation`, `resume_automation` |\n| Bots | `list_bots`, `ask_bot` |\n| Plan | `list_tasks`, `create_task`, `move_task` (a task made here waits in the person's [Inbox](./inbox.md) until they take it up) |\n\nThe usual flow is `list_hosts`, `start_session` with a focused task, `wait_for_session`, then `get_session` and `send_to_session`. `wait_for_session` and `ask_bot` hold a call for at most 300 seconds; call again while the work goes on. A session waiting on a permission or question needs the person to answer it in the console. Cloud sessions: a Cursor session takes `send_to_session` as a new run once its last run ended; Claude Code and Codex cloud sessions take none, and their transcripts are at the vendor.\n\n## Limits\n\n- Signing an MCP client in through OAuth (the console's consent screen) works, but such a grant is not tied to a workspace yet, so workspace tools refuse it. Use a pinned API token.\n- An agent in a session has no automatic Oppenheimer credential; give it a token if it should run `opp`.\n- Sessions started by `opp` are not shown as children of the session that started them.",
  },
  {
    id: "faq",
    title: "FAQ",
    description: "Short answers to common questions, such as whether a bot can run commands, where files and code live, what happens when a host goes offline or reboots, what is stored about you, which agents and providers are supported, and what is not available yet.",
    body: "## Can a bot run commands or read files?\n\nNo. A bot runs inside Oppenheimer with no shell, no files and no access to the server. When work needs a shell or a checkout, it starts a session on one of your hosts, where a coding agent does it, and reports what the session shows. See [Bots](./bots.md).\n\n## Can a bot do anything I cannot?\n\nNo. Every tool call runs as you and is checked against your current permissions. A bot never gets access to pairing hosts, connecting GitHub or Gmail, sharing sessions, API tokens or deleting bots.\n\n## Where do my code and files live?\n\nOn your hosts. A worktree session's checkout is under the host's work folder (`~/oppenheimer/` by default); a folder session works in your own folder. The agent's conversation (its transcript) also stays on the host; the console reads it from there when you open the Chat view. Oppenheimer itself keeps a session's log (tasks, prompts, states), not your checkout. Bot chats, bot memory and images sent to bots are stored in Oppenheimer.\n\n## Is anything on my machine exposed to the internet?\n\nNo. The runner opens no port; it holds one outbound connection to Oppenheimer.\n\n## What happens when a host goes offline?\n\nRunning sessions keep running in tmux on the host; the terminal says \"Host offline\" and reconnects by itself. New sessions on that host wait as Queued and start when it is back. Removing a host from Settings → Hosts stops its sessions. See [Hosts](./hosts.md).\n\n## What happens when a host reboots?\n\nSessions come back stopped, with their worktrees intact. Press **Restart** to reopen the agent where it left off. A session marked kept (`runner sessions keep`) restarts on its own.\n\n## Does a runner update interrupt my agents?\n\nNo. Sessions live in tmux, apart from the runner. The runner waits for a quiet moment to update, and terminals reconnect in a few seconds.\n\n## Which agents can a session run?\n\nClaude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Grok, Pi and Oh My Pi, or a blank terminal. Each uses the host's own installation and sign-in; you sign in once per host in the terminal. Claude Code, Codex and Cursor can also run in their vendors' clouds.\n\n## Which models can bots use?\n\nClaude through Anthropic, GPT through OpenAI or a ChatGPT subscription, Grok through xAI, and GLM through z.ai. Add keys in Settings → Model providers.\n\n## Does Oppenheimer store my GitHub token or my agents' logins?\n\nSessions get one-hour tokens scoped to their repositories, handed to git on the host and never written to disk. Your own GitHub user token is kept only when the deployment has a key for it: connecting GitHub then stores it encrypted, so the Pull requests section can act as you, and drops it when it can no longer be refreshed. Agent logins stay in each agent's own config on the host. Model provider keys you add for bots are stored encrypted and never shown again.\n\n## What happens to a session's branch when I delete it?\n\nIts branch (`oppenheimer/…`) is pushed when it has commits, then the worktree is removed. If there is work that is not pushed, deleting stops unless you choose to discard it. Oppenheimer never commits for you.\n\n## Can someone else see my session?\n\nOnly if you share it: specific people or anyone signed in with the link can watch; named people can be given write access for up to 24 hours, acting as you. See [Sessions](./sessions.md).\n\n## Can I have a team workspace?\n\nNot yet. Each workspace has one person, with no members or invitations.\n\n## How do I start work from a script or another agent?\n\nUse `opp` or the MCP server with a scoped API token. See [CLI and MCP](./cli-and-mcp.md).\n\n## Where do I find things that need me?\n\n**Plan → Inbox**, where the console opens by default. See [Inbox](./inbox.md).\n\n## What is not available yet?\n\nVirtual machines and containers as hosts; teams; Slack; people writing to a bot over mail, WhatsApp, Telegram or Slack; bot templates and group chats; the `/plan` and `/grill-me` commands; automation templates and headless runs; push guardrails; extra terminal windows in the console; pull requests from forks in review sessions.",
  },
];
